In article <1909@l.cc.purdue.edu> nvq@l.cc.purdue.edu (Michael Scholl) writes:
>In addition to plain old spell books, why not have 
>"information" books.
>For instance:
>
>Book ogf Gems-  After reading allows you to identify all Gems.
>Book of Armaments-  After reading you could identify weapons.
>Mayor's Jeweler's Catalogue (or Frobozz, etc)-  Could identify rings, etc.
>
>These books should be rare, and take a LONG time (50) to read.
>The only one I would Really like to see is the Gem one.  Theer are
>other ways of identifying most other things.

I really like this idea.  I would like to suggest a couple of
modifications:

   1) You need to keep the book, and read it whenver you are trying to
      identify the object in question.  This means it costs you
      inventory weight.

   2) For this reason, the book does not get "used up" like a scroll or
      a spellbook.

   3) A blessed reference book takes 1 turn to read and always works.

   4) An uncursed reference book takes 10-50 turns, and may not work
      (based on your intelligence and experience level).

   5) A cursed book might strike you blind - or it might lie!!
      (this would be really sneaky :-))

   6) A reference book on weapons would include the normal hit/dam
      values of the weapon in question.


